<usx version="3.0"><book code="2SA" style="id">- TJs USFM Test</book><para style="toc3">2Sam</para><para style="toc2">2 Sam</para><para style="toc1">2 Samuel</para><chapter number="1" style="c" altnumber="1 ca" pubnumber="1 cp" sid="2SA 1" /><para style="s1">This chapter and the next two chapters have lots of challenging USFM test markers and such in them.</para><para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="2SA 1:1" /><char style="bd" closed="false">usfm marker</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:1">In USFM, the usfm marker should come right after the id marker at the top of the page. According to spec, it should only occur if the USFM version is not 3.0; usfm 3.0 should never occur, and anything else like usfm 3.1 should always be present. In USX, this USFM marker should be transformed into the top-level usx marker that contains all the content of the document including the id marker, and the version from the usfm marker should be set as the version attribute on the usx marker. However, Paratext handles this marker differently. When output to USX, the usfm marker is transformed into a para marker with style="usfm", and the version is the text content of the marker. Paratext does not remove the usfm marker, and it does not set the usx marker's version to the version specified by the usfm marker; it is always 3.0.<verse eid="2SA 1:1" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="2SA 1:2" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Footnotes and crossrefs:</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:2">The footnote in this paragraph has as category without markers in its content, so it should be an attribute on the footnote<note caller="+" style="f" category="things"><char style="fr" closed="false">1:12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false">Some footnote text.</char></note> instead of its own standalone marker. There is no space after the cat marker, which is the canonical form in the spec. Note the letter before the footnote and the space after.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:2">The end footnote in this paragraph has as category without markers in its content but with a space after, so it should be an attribute on the footnote <note caller="+" style="fe" category="things"> <char style="fr" closed="false">1:12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false">More footnote text. </char><char style="fp" closed="false">Another <char style="wj">paragraph</char> in the footnote.</char></note> instead of its own standalone marker. However, Paratext seems to interpret the space after as part of the footnote text content instead of an optional space (that is not output in canonical form) on the attribute marker according to spec. Note the space before the footnote and the space after. Also note the fp marker in the footnote is a character marker that looks like a paragraph, so character markers inside it must have + in USFM 3.0.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:2">The footnote in this paragraph has a category with markers in its content, so it should be its own standalone marker <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="cat" status="unknown"><char style="wj">stuff </char></char> <char style="fr" closed="false">1:2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false">Other footnote text.</char></note>. Note the space before the footnote and the period after.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:2">The crossref in this verse has a category without markers in its content, so it should be an attribute on the crossref<note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xo">1.2</char> <char style="xt">Crossref text.</char></note>. It also has closed character markers that do not need to be closed in 3.1 even though other markers need to be closed in 3.1. Note the letter before the crossref and the period after.<verse eid="2SA 1:2" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="2SA 1:3" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Character markers:</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">In<char style="wj">si</char>de, <char style="wj">WholeWord</char>, <char style="wj">Span Words</char>, Pa<char style="wj">rt Span wor</char>ds</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">Empty character marker here: <char style="wj" /></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">w <char style="w" lemma="stuff">marker</char> with default attribute (note default attribute with normal attribute syntax gets normalized to default attribute)</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">w <char style="w" lemma="stuff ">marker</char> with a space at the end of the default attribute. Note this space is on the default attribute. With non-default attribute syntax, any space before the closing marker is removed as part of normalization.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">w <char style="w" strong="H1234,G1234" lemma="markerLemma" srcloc="Location">marker</char> with multiple attributes. Note they are not in the typical order of lemma, strong, srcloc</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="2SA 1:1">marker</char> with default attribute - note the default attribute name changes between 3.0 link-href and 3.1 href</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="2SA 1:2" link-title="My Title" link-id="3.0 link">marker</char> with multiple attributes with 3.0 names - note the attribute name changes between 3.0 and 3.1</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" href="2SA 1:3" id="3.1 link">marker</char> with multiple attributes with 3.1 names - note the attribute name changes between 3.0 and 3.1. For some reason, Paratext 9.5 (with USFM 3.0) is automatically removing the title attribute, so it is not present.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="x-user-defined:thing">marker</char> with a user-defined URI prefix. Must begin with x-.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="prj:zzz6 2SA 1:4">marker</char> with a prj URI prefix. Links to a reference in another project.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="x-prj:zzz6 2SA 1:5">marker</char> with an x-prj URI prefix. The 3.1 docs mention that this should link to another project, but the 3.1 usx.rng doesn't seem to indicate it is allowed unlike prj: which is in the 3.1 usx.rng.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="2SA 1:6" /> marker that has no text content.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="figures/platform-bible-discord.png">marker</char> with a file path in it.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">jmp <char style="jmp" link-href="#fake-named-target-id">marker</char> with a named target in it (I do not know how to define a named target or how this differs than listing an anchor in link-id).</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">Non-jmp wj <char style="wj" link-href="2SA 1:7">marker</char> with link-href on it. The 3.0 spec says this should be considered a link, and Paratext 9.5 treats it that way. However, the 3.1 spec does not indicate such.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">k <char style="k" key="something">marker</char> with key attribute (default in 3.1+; did not exist in 3.0-)</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">Empty character marker here <char style="wj" /> has a structure space in it unlike milestones which do not.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">qt <char style="qt">marker</char> that is a character marker that looks like it is a milestone.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">Nested <char style="wj">character <char style="nd">markers</char> must</char> have + in 3.0-, but it is optional in 3.1+.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">Character markers <char style="wj">inside notes<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1:3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false">Footnote text with a </char><char style="wj">character marker</char></note> inside character markers</char> are not considered nested, so it must just be the direct parent that is considered for nesting.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:3">Character <char style="wj" closed="false">markers technically must close in 3.1+, but unclosed character markers can be handled the same as in 3.0. They should automatically close at the end of the paragraph and should be labeled as not closed in USX and USJ.</char><verse eid="2SA 1:3" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="2SA 1:4" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Milestones:</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has <ms style="qt-s" /> two milestones <ms style="qt-e" /> without attributes. There are content spaces on both sides of each milestone. Note that, unlike with character markers, there is no structural space before the closing marker when there are no attributes; it is normalized out.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has <ms style="qt-s" who="TJ " />two milestones<ms style="qt-e" />, the first of which has default who attribute. There is a content space before the first milestone only. Note that there is a structural space after the opening marker when there are attributes. Also note that the space before the closing marker is part of the default attribute as with character markers.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has a <ms style="qt-s" sid="Some quote" who="TJ" />starting milestone with sid and who.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has no milestone.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has an <ms style="qt-e" eid="Some quote" />ending milestone with default eid attribute.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has an opening ts milestone <ms style="ts-s" sid="Translator's section" /> with default sid and closing ts milestone <ms style="ts-e" eid="Translator's section" /> with default eid. Note that the default attribute can have spaces in it.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">This line has t-s </para><para style="t-s" status="unknown" vid="2SA 1:4"><unmatched marker="*" /> and t-e</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:4">For some reason, Paratext does not recognize the ts <verse eid="2SA 1:4" /></para><para style="ts" status="unknown"><unmatched marker="*" />, t-s </para><para style="t-s" status="unknown"><unmatched marker="*" />, or t-e </para><para style="t-e" status="unknown"><unmatched marker="*" /> markers. ts is a standalone translator's section milestone, and I guess the others are shorthands for ts-s and ts-e. Paratext puts a structural space before the closing marker on all three even though it should not be there.</para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="2SA 1:5" /><verse eid="2SA 1:5" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="2SA 1:6" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Leading attributes</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:6">See the id marker at the top of this book for its code leading attribute</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:6">See the chapter marker at the top of this chapter for its number leading attribute.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:6">See the many verse markers in this chapter for their number leading attribute.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:6">See the Footnotes and crossrefs section for footnote and crossref caller leading attribute.<verse eid="2SA 1:6" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="2SA 1:7" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Text content attributes</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:7">periph has alt as its text content attribute, but periph is unfortunately only supported in peripheral books (FRT, BAK, INT, and OTH) in Paratext 9.5. Paratext splits these peripheral books into a separate file for each peripheral when outputting to USX. Please see the OTH book for examples of periph markers. Note that periph's id attribute cannot use default attribute syntax; it must be named.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:7">usfm, usx, and USJ markers have version as their text content attribute, but the usfm marker is unfortunately not present on this document because it uses USFM 3.0, so spec indicates it should not be present.<verse eid="2SA 1:7" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="2SA 1:8" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Custom attributes</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:8">This <char style="wj" x-custom-attribute-1="Stuff" z-custom-attribute-2="Things">character marker</char> has two custom attributes. Custom attributes are supposed to start with x or z according to spec.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:8">This <char style="wj" custom-attribute-no-prefix="Bad" custom-attribute-no-prefix-2="Attributes">character marker</char> has two unknown attributes that do not start with x or z. Paratext treats them just like custom attributes.<verse eid="2SA 1:8" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="2SA 1:9" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Attribute markers</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:9">See the Footnotes and crossrefs section for testing the cat marker on footnotes and crossrefs.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:9">See the chapter marker at the top of this chapter for testing ca and cp being applied to the chapter marker as attribute markers.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:9">See the chapter marker at the top of chapter 2 for not closing ca which leads it to be its own separate marker. Then cp is also its own separate marker afterward and can contain other markers as content.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:9">See the chapter marker at the top of chapter 3 for testing cp after the chapter marker with content in it, which should make the cp stay as its own independent marker.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:9">Note: All remaining tests of attribute markers will also have leading attributes because v and c are the only markers left with attribute markers to test, and they both have leading attributes.<verse eid="2SA 1:9" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" altnumber="10 va" pubnumber="10 vp" sid="2SA 1:10" />This verse marker has simple va and vp with no space between or after. Paratext and the spec treat this the same way: both markers turn into attributes on the marker in USX and USJ, and there are no spaces in the text content.<verse eid="2SA 1:10" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" altnumber="11 va" pubnumber="11 vp" sid="2SA 1:11" /> This verse marker has simple va and vp with no space between but with a space after. The spec indicates there is optional whitespace after attribute markers (one space is output after attribute markers except cat in canonical form), so this space should not be in the text content. However, Paratext treats all spaces after attribute markers as text content, so Paratext will output a space after the vp.<verse eid="2SA 1:11" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" altnumber="12 va" sid="2SA 1:12" /> <char style="vp">12 vp</char>This verse marker has simple va and vp with a space between but not after. This space should be ignored as part of optional structural space according to spec, but Paratext treats it as text content. As such, Paratext will output altnumber on the verse marker, but the vp will be its own separate marker.<verse eid="2SA 1:12" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="2SA 1:13" /><char style="va">13 <char style="wj">va </char></char><char style="vp">13 vp</char>This verse marker has va with marker content in it, which makes it a standalone marker. Random <char style="va">va</char> without the verse marker also becomes a standalone marker. va does not seem to be allowed to be its own standalone marker according to spec, but it works in Paratext. The vp after the va is standalone because it is no longer connected to the verse marker.<verse eid="2SA 1:13" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" altnumber="14" sid="2SA 1:14" /><char style="vp">14 <char style="wj">vp</char></char>This verse marker has vp with marker content in it, which makes it a standalone marker. Random <char style="vp">vp</char> without the verse marker also becomes a standalone marker. The va before the vp appropriately becomes altnumber on the verse marker.<verse eid="2SA 1:14" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="2SA 1:15" /><char style="va" closed="false">15 va </char><char style="vp" closed="false">15 vp This verse marker has va and vp without closing markers. Neither of these markers becomes an attribute in USX or USJ because both have closed="false" and are therefore not simple markers.</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:15">This paragraph contains a random <char style="ca">ca with an <char style="wj">inline marker inside</char></char> not after the chapter marker, which becomes a standalone marker. ca does not seem to be allowed to be its own standalone marker according to spec, but it works in Paratext.<verse eid="2SA 1:15" /></para><para style="cp">This paragraph is a random cp with an <char style="wj">inline marker inside</char> not after the chapter marker, which becomes a standalone marker.</para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="2SA 1:16" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Whitespace</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:16">Tilde should be a NBSP in USX and USJ</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:16">Double slash should be an optbreak in USX and USJ. Here <optbreak /> is one with spaces around it. Here<optbreak />is one without spaces around it.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:16">Paratext 9.5's unformatted view leaves a space after the number on verse markers, but the standard view does not. This seems like a bug in the unformatted view.<verse eid="2SA 1:16" /></para><para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="2SA 1:17" /></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:17">TODO: add some whitespace character tests<verse eid="2SA 1:17" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="2SA 1:18" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Figures</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:18">There is a figure here <figure style="fig" file="platform-bible-discord.png" size="col" ref="1.13" /> . It has a space on both sides. Only the three required attributes are present and in the order in which they are listed in the spec. The attribute src in USFM should be file in USX and USJ.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:18">There is a figure here<figure style="fig" alt="Description Here" file="platform-bible-discord.png" size="span" loc="Location here" copy="Copyright here" ref="1.13">Caption Here</figure>. It has no spaces around it. All six attributes are present and are not in the order in which they are listed in the spec. The attribute src in USFM should be file in USX and USJ.<verse eid="2SA 1:18" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="2SA 1:19" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Lists</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:19">Following is a normal list with a header, entries, and footer. It has – at the start of each line like in the documentation example.</para><para style="lh" vid="2SA 1:19">Header for the <char style="wj">normal</char> list:</para><para style="li1" vid="2SA 1:19">–First <char style="wj">list</char> item</para><para style="li2" vid="2SA 1:19">–Subpoint on the first list item</para><para style="li3" vid="2SA 1:19">–Subpoint on the <char style="wj">subpoint</char> on the first list item</para><para style="li1" vid="2SA 1:19">–Second list item</para><para style="li1" vid="2SA 1:19">–Third list item</para><para style="lf" vid="2SA 1:19">Footer for the normal list.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:19">Following is an embedded list with just entries.</para><para style="lim1" vid="2SA 1:19">First embedded list item</para><para style="lim1" vid="2SA 1:19">Second embedded list item</para><para style="lim1" vid="2SA 1:19">Third embedded list item</para><para style="lim2" vid="2SA 1:19">Subpoint on the third embedded list item</para><para style="lim3" vid="2SA 1:19">Subpoint on the subpoint on the third embedded list item<verse eid="2SA 1:19" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="2SA 1:20" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Tables</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:20">Following is a table with various kinds of cells and a header row. Paratext only supports th1 through th12 because that is all that is in usfm.sty. Note that USFM 3.1 does not require closing markers on table cells.</para><table><row style="tr"><cell style="th1" align="start">Header 1</cell><cell style="th2" align="start">Header 2 space after </cell><cell style="thc3" align="center" colspan="2">Header 3-4 centered</cell><cell style="thr5" align="end">Header 5 right</cell></row><row style="tr"><cell style="tc1" align="start">Row 1 cell 1</cell><cell style="tc2" align="start">Row 1 cell 2 space after </cell><cell style="thc3" align="center">Row 1 cell 3 centered</cell><cell style="thr4" align="end" colspan="2">Row 1 cell 4-5 right</cell></row><row style="tr"><cell style="tcr1" align="end" colspan="4">Row 2 cell 1-4 right</cell><cell style="tc5" align="start">Row 2 cell 5</cell></row></table><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:20">Following is a table with 6 columns but no header row. Paratext does not support tc13 or higher and therefore makes two separate tables of one row each on this line, but it interestingly allows having multiple tc5 cells in one row. The spec does not appear to impose any limits on the column number.</para><table><row style="tr"><cell style="tc1" align="start">r1c1</cell><cell style="tc2" align="start">r1c2 with closed wj <char style="wj">marker</char> space after </cell><cell style="tc3" align="start">r1c3</cell><cell style="tc4" align="start">r1c4</cell><cell style="tc5" align="start">r1c5 </cell><cell style="tc5" align="start">r1c5 again </cell></row></table><para style="tc13" status="unknown" vid="2SA 1:20">r1c13</para><table><row style="tr"><cell style="tc1" align="start">r2c1</cell><cell style="tc2" align="start">r2c2 with unclosed wj <char style="wj" closed="false">marker space after </char></cell><cell style="tc3" align="start">r2c3</cell><cell style="tc4" align="start">r2c4</cell><cell style="tc5" align="start">r2c5</cell><cell style="tc5" align="start">r2c5 again </cell></row></table><para style="tc13" status="unknown" vid="2SA 1:20">r2c13<verse eid="2SA 1:20" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="2SA 1:21" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Periphs</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:21">periph is unfortunately only supported in peripheral books (FRT, BAK, INT, and OTH) in Paratext 9.5. Paratext splits these peripheral books into a separate file for each peripheral when outputting to USX. Please see the OTH book for examples of periph markers.<verse eid="2SA 1:21" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="2SA 1:22" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Custom markers</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:22">Following is a custom marker that is unknown (not in custom.sty or markers.ext). Paratext translates unknown markers to para type markers in USX. Custom markers must start with z. <verse eid="2SA 1:22" /></para><para style="zUnknownCustomMarker" status="unknown">This text is <char style="wj">in the custom</char> marker.</para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="23-24" style="v" sid="2SA 1:23-24" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Verse range</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:23-24">This is a verse range.<verse eid="2SA 1:23-24" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="2SA 1:25" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Refs</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:25">Paratext 9.5 standard view does not support entering an independent ref marker; it just replaces the ref with its text content. However, it will allow an unclosed ref marker (the contents are all put in the text content of the marker; the default attribute loc does not work): </para><para style="ref" status="unknown" vid="2SA 1:25">2Sam 1:1|REV 1:1</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:25">Also, if you enter a ref marker in the unformatted view and don't touch it in the standard view, it will work:</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:25" /><para style="ref" status="unknown" vid="2SA 1:25">2Sam 1:1|2SA 1:1<unmatched marker="ref*" /></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:25">This crossref<note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xo" closed="false">1:21 </char><char style="xt" closed="false">2Sam 1:1; 2Sam 1:2-3.</char></note> has a properly filled out xt marker. Paratext generates ref markers around each Scripture reference in the xt when outputting USX. The ref marker text content is the localized reference, and the loc attribute (default) is the canonized reference. When translated back to USFM, the refs inside the xt need to be removed; only the text content should remain.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:25">This paragraph has a properly filled out xt marker: <char style="xt">2Sam 1:2; 2Sam 1:3</char>. Paratext generates ref markers around each Scripture reference in the xt when outputting USX the same way as it does for xt markers in crossrefs as detailed in the previous paragraph.<verse eid="2SA 1:25" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="2SA 1:26" /><char style="bd" closed="false">Sidebars</char></para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:26">There is a closed sidebar after this paragraph. It has the category "Test Category". Note that Paratext's Standard view normalizes out any space before the closing cat marker, so there cannot be trailing space on the category unless you use a different view. It seems the spec allows trailing space there as part of the text content of the marker.</para><sidebar style="esb" category="Test Category"><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:26">This paragraph is in the sidebar. The sidebar can contain many things like <char style="wj">character markers</char>, <ms style="qt1-s" /> milestones <ms style="qt1-e" />, and more paragraphs.</para><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:26">This is a second paragraph in the sidebar. The sidebar will end at esbe marker.</para></sidebar><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:26">There is a sidebar that is not closed after this paragraph. The entire rest of the chapter will be in this sidebar because sidebars automatically close at the end of the chapter.</para><sidebar style="esb" closed="false"><para style="p" vid="2SA 1:26">This paragraph is in the sidebar.<verse eid="2SA 1:26" /></para><para style="b" /><para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="2SA 1:27" />This is <char style="wj">still</char> in the sidebar.<verse eid="2SA 1:27" /></para></sidebar><chapter eid="2SA 1" /></usx>